 ],
 "literal": "For the songstress to wear an unfading garland, / on the bard's head, a pool-not-bloomed / fire-resembling lotus great flower to sway, / having put a black male goat, a dark male animal, on the red fire, / with seasoning, well-cooked, wide-spaced fat slices of meat, / drinking liquor, with red mouths, moving their tongues, / both drinking and eating, and giving to those who ask, / let us rejoice, come, O valiant warrior! / Becoming rare things and appropriate things, O great one, / splitting the earth, descended dangling many roots / from the hollow of the ancient tree, suddenly hooting, / the rock horned owl bird's unceasing / burial-urn-filled great crematorium forest is reached on that day indeed.",
 "translation": "Come, O valiant warrior, let us rejoice! Let us drink liquor, move our tongues with red mouths, eat and chew well-seasoned, fat slices of meat cooked over the red fire from a black male goat, and give generously to all who ask. Let the songstress wear an unfading garland, and let the great, fire-like golden lotus flower sway on the bard's head. For on that inevitable day, O great lord, we must reach the vast crematorium forest filled with burial urns, where the rock horned owl hoots suddenly from the hollow of an ancient tree whose many dangling roots split the earth. On that day, all these pleasures will become rare and impossible.",
 "modern": "வாடாத மாலையைப் பாடினி அணியவும், பாணனின் தலையில் குளத்தில் பூக்காத நெருப்புப் போன்ற பொன்னாலான தாமரைப் பெருமலர் அசையவும், கரிய ஆட்டுக் கடாவைச் செந்நெருப்பில் வாட்டி, மசாலா சேர்த்து நன்கு வெந்த அகன்ற கொழுத்த இறைச்சித் துண்டுகளை, மதுவருந்திச் சிவந்த வாயால் நாவை அசைத்து உண்டும் தின்றும், இரப்போர்க்கு ஈந்தும் நாம் மகிழ்வோம்; வாராய் போர் வீரனே! நிலத்தைப் பிளந்து ஆழமாக இறங்கிய பல விழுதுகளை உடைய பழமையான மரத்தின் பொந்திலிருந்து திடீரெனக் கூவும் கூகையாகிய ஆந்தை ஓயாமல் கத்தும், ஈமத்தாழிகள் நிறைந்த பெரிய சுடுகாட்டை நாம் அடையும் நாளில், பெருமானே, இந்த இன்பங்கள் யாவும் அரியனவாகிவிடும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puram genre, specifically under the theme of 'Peruṅkāñci' (the great instability of life) in the 'Potuviyal' tiṇai. The poet, Kūkaik Kōriyār, addresses a valiant warrior, urging him to enjoy the pleasures of life while he can. He describes a feast of roasted goat meat and liquor, shared generously with bards and songstresses who are adorned with golden lotus flowers. This vibrant celebration of life is starkly contrasted with the grim reality of death. The poet reminds the warrior that everyone must eventually reach the vast crematorium forest, marked by burial urns and the eerie hooting of owls from ancient hollow trees. The transition from the warmth of the feast to the coldness of the graveyard emphasizes the transience of youth, power, and earthly joys.",
